e" "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $4\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(8,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(8,0),$ we have that $r(0)=8i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=8.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=8$ and $\\omega =4\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdca8b5d8aa64b959fc7306a04e3d1a5f55216bda2d412c0fa0b39360e67cc1"} {"question" "q-97632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j)"] ["math" "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j)"] ["math" "r(t)=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)"] ["math" "r(t)=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)"] ["math" "r(t)=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r(t)=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $4\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=4\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =1\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=4\\cdot |1|=4\\text{ m/s}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdca8b5d8aa64b959fc7306a04e3d1a5f55216bda2d412c0fa0b39360e67cc1"} {"question" "q-97639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "2\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "36\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "4\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "8\\text{ m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\text{ m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $8\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=3$ and $\\omega =8\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\\,j \\\\  & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\,j \\\\  & =-24\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdca8b5d8aa64b959fc7306a04e3d1a5f55216bda2d412c0fa0b39360e67cc1"} {"question" "q-97663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12\\,i+12\\,j"] ["math" "-12\\,j"] ["math" "-24\\,j"] ["math" "8\\,i"] ["math" "8\\,i-8\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-24\\,j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡3t+36{cos}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡3t+{cos}^{2}⁡3t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $6\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=2\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=2\\cdot |3|=6\\text{ m/s}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdca8b5d8aa64b959fc7306a04e3d1a5f55216bda2d412c0fa0b39360e67cc1"} {"question" "q-97541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "2\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "36\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "3\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "6\\text{ m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6\\text{ m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(7,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=7$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,j \\\\  & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{14\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{14}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i+7\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdca8b5d8aa64b959fc7306a04e3d1a5f55216bda2d412c0fa0b39360e67cc1"} {"question" "q-97665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-14\\sqrt{3}\\,i+14j"] ["math" "-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i"] ["math" "-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i+7j"] ["math" "7\\,i-7\\sqrt{3}j"] ["math" "7j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i+7j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,i+6\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-6\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t)^{2}+(6\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\pi t+36{\\pi }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡2\\pi t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡2\\pi t+{cos}^{2}⁡2\\pi t)} \\\\  & =6\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $6\\pi \\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =2\\pi \\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=3\\cdot |2\\pi |=6\\pi \\text{ m/s}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdca8b5d8aa64b959fc7306a04e3d1a5f55216bda2d412c0fa0b39360e67cc1"} {"question" "q-97642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\pi \\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "36\\pi \\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "36\\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "6\\pi \\text{ m/s}"] ["math" "6\\text{ m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6\\pi \\text{ m/s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $4\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(5,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=5$ and $\\omega =4\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+20\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-20\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+20\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-80\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-80\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,j \\\\  & =-80\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =-80\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdca8b5d8aa64b959fc7306a04e3d1a5f55216bda2d412c0fa0b39360e67cc1"} {"question" "q-97673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-40\\,i"] ["math" "-40\\,i-40\\,j"] ["math" "-80\\,i"] ["math" "-80\\,i+80\\,j"] ["math" "80\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-80\\,i"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(4,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=4$ and $\\omega =3\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =36\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdca8b5d8aa64b959fc7306a04e3d1a5f55216bda2d412c0fa0b39360e67cc1"} {"question" "q-97672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12\\,i-12\\,j"] ["math" "-36\\,i+36\\,j"] ["math" "12\\,i"] ["math" "36\\,i"] ["math" "36\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "36\\,i"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,j \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{2}\\,i-\\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-6\\,i-6\\sqrt{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdca8b5d8aa64b959fc7306a04e3d1a5f55216bda2d412c0fa0b39360e67cc1"} {"question" "q-97568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12\\sqrt{3}\\,i-12\\,j"] ["math" "-6\\,i"] ["math" "-6\\,i-6\\,j"] ["math" "-6\\,i-6\\sqrt{3}\\,j"] ["math" "-6\\sqrt{3}\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6\\,i-6\\sqrt{3}\\,j"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $1\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(2,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=2$ and $\\omega =1\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{2}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdca8b5d8aa64b959fc7306a04e3d1a5f55216bda2d412c0fa0b39360e67cc1"} {"question" "q-97671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}i+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,j"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j"] ["math" "\\,i-\\sqrt{3}\\,j"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)^{2}+(-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{16}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡4t+{16}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{16}^{2}} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=1\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =4\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={4}^{2}\\cdot 1=16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6bdca8b5d8aa64b959fc7306a04e3d1a5f55216bda2d412c0fa0b39360e67cc1"} {"question" "q-97668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"] ["math" "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"] ["math" "4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"] ["math" "64\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"] ["math" "8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "q-97668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4de5674ea680dd236f5b69d6a53d6a92c007bf6d9a46661b876ea41984f43e"} {"question" "e-8642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3 \\textrm{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(7,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/example-8642.json" "file_sha256" "cd02f203369878d3c9ecdf41b657f6c0ddfd38bb2b81abe353f5030e71fdae27"} {"question" "e-8642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos \\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin \\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(7,0),$ we have that $\\mathbf r(0)=\\,7\\mathbf i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=7.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=7$ and $\\omega= -3  \\textrm{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3t)\\,j) \\\\  & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/example-8642.json" "file_sha256" "cd02f203369878d3c9ecdf41b657f6c0ddfd38bb2b81abe353f5030e71fdae27"} {"question" "e-8643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 5\\cos \\pi t \\, \\mathbf i + 5\\sin \\pi t \\,\\mathbf j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/example-8643.json" "file_sha256" "41b36c6eee58fb305f1fab5e81eb4278839d030fc4e516419f9aed8510fa515d"} {"question" "e-8643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)^{2}+(5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+25{\\pi }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t)} \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the object's speed is $5 \\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = R|\\omega|,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=5\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = \\pi \\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = 5\\cdot |\\pi| = 5\\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/example-8643.json" "file_sha256" "41b36c6eee58fb305f1fab5e81eb4278839d030fc4e516419f9aed8510fa515d"} {"question" "e-8644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 4(\\cos 3t\\,  \\mathbf i + \\sin 3t\\, \\mathbf j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/example-8644.json" "file_sha256" "d1e5a9ea8bb914c04c6e97aaf7b7e0c6f5076f838ec80bc014dabee282586563"} {"question" "e-8644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡3t+{36}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}} \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nNote that $||\\mathbf a||$ is constant, but $\\mathbf a$ is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2\\mathbf r \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad ||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2||\\mathbf r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=4\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = 3^2\\cdot |4| = 36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064391/example-8644.json" "file_sha256" "d1e5a9ea8bb914c04c6e97aaf7b7e0c6f5076f838ec80bc014dabee282586563"}] "usage" {"e-8643" [] "e-8644" [] "q-97541" [] "e-8642" [] "q-97568" []} "basis" 2145 "retractions" [[17592186172577 :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t) = 2\\cos 3ti + 2\\sin 3tj$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds."] [17592186172580 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2rad/s$. Given that the particle is located at $(3, 0)$ when $t = 0$, find the acceleration of the particle at $t = \\frac{π}{6}$ seconds."] [17592186171780 :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3 \\textrm{rad/s}$. Given that the object is at the position $(7,0)$ when $t=0$, find the position function of the particle."] [17592186171780 :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos \\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin \\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O$, and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0$.\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(7,0)$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(0)=\\,7\\mathbf i\n$$\n\nand we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=7$.\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=7$ and\n\n$$\n\\omega= -3 \\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\n(negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= 7(\\cos (-3t)i + \\sin (-3t)j) \\\\\n&= 7(\\cos 3ti - \\sin 3tj)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186171781 :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 5\\cos \\pi t \\, \\mathbf i + 5\\sin \\pi t \\,\\mathbf j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds."] [17592186171781 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= (r(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (5\\cos πti + 5\\sin πtj)^{′} \\\\\n&=-5π\\sin πti + 5π\\cos πtj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mid v(t) \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-5π\\sin πt)^{2} + (5π\\cos πt)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25π^{2}\\sin^{2} πt + 25π^{2}\\cos^{2} πt} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25π^{2}(\\sin^{2} πt + \\cos^{2} πt)} \\\\\n&= 5π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the object's speed is $5 \\pi \\textrm{m/s}$.\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid = R \\mid \\omega \\mid\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nR=5\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nand the angular frequency\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\pi \\,\\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid = 5\\cdot \\mid \\pi \\mid = 5\\pi \\textrm{m/s}\n$$"] [17592186171782 :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 4(\\cos 3t\\, \\mathbf i + \\sin 3t\\, \\mathbf j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds."] [17592186171782 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= (r(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (4(\\cos 3ti + \\sin 3tj))^{′} \\\\\n&=-12\\sin 3ti + 12\\cos 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= (v(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (-12\\sin 3ti + 12\\cos 3tj)^{′} \\\\\n&=-36\\cos 3ti - 36\\sin 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mid a(t) \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-36\\cos 3t)^{2} + (-36\\sin 3t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36^{2}\\cos^{2} 3t + 36^{2}\\sin^{2} 3t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36^{2}} \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nNote that $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid$ is constant, but $\\mathbf a$ is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2\\mathbf r \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad \\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid = \\omega^2 \\mid \\mid \\mathbf r \\mid \\mid\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\na=4\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nand the angular frequency\n\n$$\n\\omega = 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid = 3^2\\cdot \\mid 4 \\mid = 36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186171428 17592186171429 17592186171430 17592186171431 17592186171432 17592186171446 17592186171447 17592186171448 17592186171449 17592186171450] "immutable_fields" [[17592186171427 :answer-field/correct] [17592186171427 :answer-field/id] [17592186171427 :answer-field/key] [17592186171427 :answer-field/type] [17592186171445 :answer-field/correct] [17592186171445 :answer-field/id] [17592186171445 :answer-field/key] [17592186171445 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
